+{"at":"2026-10-01T05:59:21.110Z","model":"claude-opus-5-5","foul":"foul-contrast","planted_foul":"low contrast — the \"Save changes\" button text is light gray on a near-white background","found_planted":true,"is_error":false,"cost_usd":0.29951720000000004,"response":"**What it is:** a phone-width \"Profile\" settings page. It has a header with the Framework logo and a menu button, a big bold \"Profile\" heading, one \"Display name\" text field filled in with \"Alex Rivera\", and a fixed bottom bar holding a \"•••\" button and an orange sparkle button.\n\n**Worst fouls, most serious first:**\n\n1. **You can barely see the text field's outline.** The box around \"Alex Rivera\" is a thin, pale grey line on a page that's almost the same pale grey. The field also has no fill of its own, so it shares the page's background. Together that makes it read as loose text, not something you can click and type into. Form-control borders need at least 3:1 contrast against what's around them (the WCAG 1.4.11 rule), and this looks well under that. Give the field a white fill, a darker border, or both.\n\n2. **The bottom bar covers the field.** The bar is pinned to the bottom of the screen and sits right on the field's lower edge, so the field's bottom border is hidden behind it. The page's main content is being clipped by its own chrome. The content needs bottom padding equal to the bar's height.\n\n3. **The label is too far from its field.** There's about 40px between \"Display name\" and its box. That's nearly as much as between the heading and the label, so the label floats instead of belonging to the field. Labels should sit about 4–8px above their input.\n\n4. **The \"•••\" icon is faint and neither button says what it does.** The dots are small and mid-grey, the weakest-contrast thing on the screen. They also look much weaker than the bright orange sparkle next to them. Neither button has a text label, so a newcomer can't tell what either one does.\n\n5. **The hierarchy is lopsided.** The very heavy \"Profile\" heading dominates a page whose only actual content is one quiet field. The field, which is what you came here to use, is the weakest thing on screen. Making the field stronger (fix 1) matters more than shrinking the heading, but the gap between the two is too big.\n\nI judged this from the screenshot alone. I didn't open the `page.js` next to it, so none of these points is based on how the test was built. I also couldn't measure exact pixel colours because Python's image library (PIL) isn't installed here, so the contrast calls are by eye."}
+{"at":"2026-10-01T05:59:51.062Z","model":"claude-opus-5-5","foul":"foul-overflow","planted_foul":"content overflows the 400px viewport — the status line runs off the right edge","found_planted":true,"is_error":false,"cost_usd":0.2769408,"response":"This is a phone-width screen (about 400px) for a \"Status panel\" page. It has a white top bar with the orange Framework logo and a hamburger menu, a large bold heading, two status lines on a light grey background, and a bottom bar with a \"•••\" button and an orange sparkle button.\n\n**Worst fouls, most serious first:**\n\n1. **The second status line runs off the right edge.** It stops mid-sentence at \"…wider than\" because its fixed width is wider than the screen. Readers lose the end of the message, and the page probably scrolls sideways too. This is the main foul. The line should wrap: drop the fixed width, or use `max-width: 100%`.\n2. **Nothing on the page actually shows a status.** \"All systems normal\" is plain grey text, styled exactly like the broken line below it. There's no colour, icon or badge to separate healthy from failing, so a reader can't check the status at a glance.\n3. **The bottom-bar buttons have no labels and are placed unevenly.** \"•••\" sits at about a quarter of the width and the sparkle at about three quarters, with no text under either. A newcomer can't tell what they do. Together with the top bar, the bars also take about a third of this short screen.\n4. **The content doesn't line up with the header.** The heading and text start about 28px from the left, but the logo starts about 14px in, so the page has two left edges.\n5. **The type scale and spacing are off.** The roughly 38px heavy heading is far too loud for two lines of content on a phone. The gaps (about 55px under the heading, about 45px between lines) are large enough that the two lines look like separate sections rather than one list.\n6. **The orange is weak on white.** The light orange logo mark and the sparkle look below the 3:1 contrast that icons need. It's minor, but the sparkle is the main action and it's also the faintest thing in that bar."}
+{"at":"2026-10-01T06:00:13.167Z","model":"claude-opus-5-5","foul":"foul-padding","planted_foul":"zero padding — the notice text touches its own box border on every side","found_planted":true,"is_error":false,"cost_usd":0.2725792,"response":"This is a short page called \"Notice\" on a mobile-width Framework site. It has a header with a hamburger menu, a big \"Notice\" heading, a small \"Account settings\" label, and a yellow warning box that says \"Your subscription renews in 3 days. Update your payment method to avoid interruption.\" A bottom bar holds a \"•••\" button and an orange sparkle icon.\n\n**Worst fouls, most serious first:**\n\n1. **The warning box has no padding.** The text sits right against the orange border on all four sides. \"Your\" touches the left edge, and the second line, \"interruption.\", nearly rests on the bottom border. This is the most obvious fault. It makes the box look broken, not designed. It needs roughly 12–16px of padding inside.\n\n2. **The warning gives no way to act.** It says to \"Update your payment method\", but that phrase isn't a link and there's no button. A warning that asks for an action should include the way to take it.\n\n3. **\"Account settings\" doesn't clearly belong to anything.** It's a small, plain line between the heading and the box, with no styling to say what it is. It could be a breadcrumb, a subtitle or a section label. It sits much closer to the box than to the heading, so it reads like a caption for the warning, which is probably wrong.\n\n4. **The spacing has no rhythm.** There's a large gap between \"Notice\" and \"Account settings\" and a small one between the label and the box. Below the box, about 75px of empty grey runs down to the bottom bar. The page feels unfinished rather than calm.\n\n5. **The box width doesn't line up with anything.** It stops at about 605px. That isn't full width and doesn't match the heading or the label, so there's a ragged empty strip on the right. It should either fill the content column or follow the same measure as the text.\n\n6. **The heading overpowers the content.** \"Notice\" is huge and very heavy, and the warning text is much smaller. That size gap is too big for a one-message page, and the heading says less than the box does. Something like \"Subscription renewal\" would tell the reader more.\n\n7. **The bottom bar is unclear.** The \"•••\" and the sparkle are placed off-centre with no labels, so a reader can't tell what either one does."}
