+{"decision":{"question":"Which architecture best prevents stale-badge bugs in a three-class notification system?","id":"d-notif-arch-haiku","rank":1,"options":[{"id":"a","text":"NoticeTray manages count directly","caveats":["Badge stays stale if list mutates outside tray.add()"]},{"id":"b","text":"NoticeList fires change event","caveats":["Reuses .on() for custom events, one more concept to learn"]},{"id":"c","text":"Notice dismisses itself, tray reads DOM","caveats":["Badge stays stale if list mutates outside tray.notify()"]}],"recommended":"b","confidence":0.95,"why":"Option B is the only choice where the stale-badge bug is structurally impossible. The event fires from the one place data changes, so any caller sees the update. Options A and C both require discipline—remembering to call an update line—which breaks in production. The cost is low: .on() already exists for clicks, so this reuses a familiar pattern.","status":"decided","decided_by":"system"}}
